w much sales did PEP lose with KFC moving to Coke? How does PEP's $138.8 price compare with targets? \n\n## PepsiCo, Inc. Overview Beverages - Non-Alcoholic / Consumer Defensive\n\nPepsiCo, Inc. engages in the manufacture, marketing, distribution, and sale of various beverages and convenient foods worldwide. The company operates through six segments: PepsiCo Foods North America; PepsiCo Beverages North America; International Beverages Franchise; Europe, Middle East and Africa; Latin America Foods; and Asia Pacific Foods. It offers cereals, chips, dips, granola bars, oatmeal, pasta, rice, and syrups and mixes; refrigerated dips and spreads; beverage concentrates, fountain syrups, and finished goods; and ready-to-drink tea and coffee products. The company also provides SodaStream sparkling water makers and related products, as well as various dairy products under the Agusha, Chudo, and Domik v Derevne brands. It serves wholesale and other distributors, foodservice customers, grocery stores, drug stores, convenience stores, discount/dollar stores, mass merchandisers, membership stores, hard discounters, e-commerce retailers and authorized independent bottlers, and others through a network of direct-store-delivery, customer warehouse, and distributor networks, as well as directly to consumers through e-commerce platforms and retailers. PepsiCo, Inc. was founded in 1898 and is based in Purchase, New York.\n\nwww.pepsico.com \n\n306,000\n\n### Full Time Employees\n\nDecember 27\n\n### Fiscal Year Ends\n\nConsumer Defensive \n\n### Sector\n\nBeverages - Non-Alcoholic \n\n### Industry\n\n### How Eric Skae built a $100M pasta sauce empire: Carbone Fine Food\n\n### Performance Overview\n\nTrailing total returns as of 10/23/2026, which may include dividends or other distributions. Benchmark is S&P 500 (^GSPC) .\n\n### YTD Return\n\nPEP 2.52% \n\nS&P 500 (^GSPC) 11.37%\n\n### 1-Year Return\n\nPEP 6.76% \n\nS&P 500 (^GSPC) 13.14%\n\n### 3-Year Return\n\nPEP 5.49% \n\nS&P 500 (^GSPC) 80.78%\n\n### 5-Year Return\n\nPEP 0.19% \n\nS&P 500 (^GSPC) 67.74%\n\n### Revenue vs. Earnings \n\nRevenue 25.27B\n\nEarnings 3.05B\n\nProfit Margin 12.06%\n\nQ4\n\nFY25\n\nQ1\n\nFY26\n\nQ2\n\nFY26\n\nQ3\n\nFY26\n\nTop Analyst \n\nMorgan Stanley\n\n51/100\n\nEqual-Weight \n\n### Analyst Price Targets\n\n106.00 Low\n\n141.59 Average\n\n135.77 Current\n\n169.00 High\n\n### Analyst Recommendations\n\n Strong Buy Buy Hold Underperform Sell \n\n### Latest Rating\n\nDate 10/12/2026 Analyst Barclays Rating Action Maintains Rating Equal-Weight Price Action Lowers Price Target 133 -> 126\n\nAs of 9/16/2026\n\n- Market Cap \n\n183.36B \n- Enterprise Value \n\n225.86B \n- Trailing P/E \n\n17.61 \n- Forward P/E \n\n14.86 \n- PEG Ratio (5yr expected) \n\n1.38 \n- Price/Sales (ttm)\n\n1.90 \n- Price/Book (mrq)\n\n8.30 \n- Enterprise Value/Revenue \n\n2.33 \n- Enterprise Value/EBITDA \n\n12.12 \n\n#### Profitability and Income Statement\n\n- Profit Margin \n\n11.09%\n- Return on Assets (ttm)\n\n9.02%\n- Return on Equity (ttm)\n\n52.19%\n- Revenue (ttm)\n\n98.24B\n- Net Income Avi to Common (ttm)\n\n10.9B\n- Diluted EPS (ttm)\n\n7.95\n\n#### Balance Sheet and Cash Flow\n\n- Total Cash (mrq)\n\n11.16B\n- Total Debt/Equity (mrq)\n\n230.72%\n- Levered Free Cash Flow (ttm)\n\n9.04B\n\nSelect to analyze similar companies using key performance metrics; select up to 4 stocks.\n\nCoca-Cola Europacific Partners PLC\n\nGURU.TO\n\nGURU Organic Energy Corp.\n\nMkt Cap CAD 82.823M Industry Beverages—Non-Alcoholic\n\n### Fair Value\n\nFair Value is the appropriate price for the shares of a company, based on its earnings and growth rate also interpreted as when P/E Ratio = Growth Rate. Estimated return represents the projected annual return you might expect after purchasing shares in the company and holding them over the default time horizon of 5 years, based on the EPS growth rate that we have projected. Learn more \n\n135.77 Current\n\n### Dividend Score\n\nThe distribution of earnings a company pays back to its shareholders based off the company’s profits. Usually paid out on a quarterly basis in the form of cash or additional stocks.\n\nSector Avg.\n\n100 High\n\n### Hiring Score\n\nA company’s hiring trends in key indicators can indicate how well the company is doing in terms of growing the company and where they are currently prioritizing.\n\nSector Avg.\n\n100 High\n\n### Insider Sentiment Score\n\nVicker’s Insider Sentiment provides insights into trades made by insiders. This can be used to gain valuable insights and as a reflection of the prospect of the company based on insider trading.\n\nSector Avg.\n\n100 High\n\n- ### Looking for a favorable entry point\n\nPepsiCo, founded in 1898, produces and sells food, snacks, and beverages around the world. The company's brands include Lay's, Santitas, Ruffles, Doritos, Tostitos, Cheetos, Quaker Oatmeal, and Rice-A-Roni; its beverage portfolio includes Pepsi, Mountain Dew, Gatorade, Lipton, Brisk, and various bottled-water products. The company also provides tea and coffee products through a joint venture with Starbucks and Unilever. The company has approximately 306,000 employees. The shares are a component of the S&P 500.\n\nArgus• 12 days ago \n- ### Fresh inflation data will be released this week, but the onset of earnings\n\nFresh inflation data will be released this week, but the onset of earnings report likely will take center stage in the form of updates from the big banks. Last week, the Dow Jones Industrial Average, the S&P 500, and the Nasdaq all gained about 1%. Year to date, the DJIA is up 7%, the S&P 500 14%, and the Nasdaq 18%. On the economic calendar, two important inflation reports top the list this week. The Consumer Price Index (CPI) will be released on Wednesday, followed by the Producer Price Index (PPI) on Thursday. Both of those reports will be of interest to the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) as the members of that body ponder their next decision about the path of interest rates (which will come on October 28). The odds related to a possible rate increase have been flipping back and forth -- but as of Friday, the odds for no move were 81%. On the earnings calendar, JPMorgan Chase, Goldman Sachs, Wells Fargo, Citigroup, Johnson & Johnson, and UnitedHealth all report on Tuesday. Bank of America, Morgan Stanley, BlackRock, State Street Bank, and ExxonMobil are on the list for Wednesday. Taiwan Semiconductor, Charles Schwab, Bank of New York Mellon, and PNC Financial are on Thursday, and Traveler's Companies, Truist Financial, M&T Bank, and Citizens Financial are on Friday. Turning to other economic metrics, gas prices fell 11 cents last week, to an average of $4.35 per gallon for regular gas. Elsewhere, the Atlanta Fed GDPNow forecast calls for GDP growth of 3.6% in the third quarter. This forecast has been bouncing around over the last few weeks. The Cleveland Fed Inflation Nowcast CPI outlook remains at 3.6% for September and October. Mortgage rates popped up another 12 basis points last week, with the average 30-year fixed-rate mortgage now at 7.40%, according to FreddieMac. Taking a deeper dive into performance so far in 2026, a leading industrialized global stock market index, the ETF EFA, is up 7% year to date, while the leading emerging market ETF (EEM) is up 22%. U.S. growth stocks are up 7% when looking at ETF IWF, and value stocks (IWD) are up 20%. Crude oil prices continue to be volatile. On Friday, oil was at about $91 per barrel, up 57% for the year. In other asset classes for the year to date, AGG bonds are down 6%, gold is down 4%, and Bitcoin is down 6%. The U.S. dollar is up 4%, tracking DXY. The VIX Volatility Index was at about 15 on Friday, below its historical average of 20. Turning to sector performance, the list from first to worst so far in 2026 is Energy (+46%), Information Technology (+37%), Industrial (+9%), Materials (+9%), Healthcare (+9%), Consumer Staples (+7%), Real Estate (+1%), Financials (-1%), Utilities (-4%), Communication Services (-5%), and Consumer Discretionary (-6%).\n\nArgus• 12 days ago \n- ### US Large Cap Pick List - October 2026\n\nThis pick list highlights constituents of the Morningstar US Large Cap Index that we believe offer investors the best risk-adjusted return prospects. Stocks of large-cap companies where neither growth nor value characteristics predominate. Stocks in the top 70% of the capitalization of the U.S. equity market are defined as large cap.\n\nMorningstar• 15 days ago \n- ### PepsiCo Earnings: Results Outpace Muted Expectations, but Patience for Improvement Waning\n\nPepsiCo is a global leader in snacks and beverages, owning well-known household brands including Pepsi, Mountain Dew, Gatorade, Lay’s, Cheetos, and Doritos, among others. The company dominates the global savory snacks market and also ranks as the second-largest beverage provider in the world (behind Coca-Cola) with diversified exposure to carbonated soft drinks, or CSD, as well as water, sports, and energy drink offerings. Convenience foods account for approximately 58% of its total revenue, with beverages making up the rest. Pepsi owns the bulk of its manufacturing and distribution capacity in the US, but uses bottlers overseas for beverages. International markets made up 41% of total sales and 46% of operating profits before corporate expenses in 2025. \n\nMorningstar• 16 days ago","title":"PepsiCo, Inc. (PEP) Stock Price, News, Quote & History - Yahoo Finance","url":"https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/PEP/"},{"author":null,"id":"https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/MDLZ/","text":"Mondelez International, Inc. (MDLZ) Stock Price, News, Quote & History - Yahoo Finance\n\n# Mondelez International, Inc. (MDLZ)\n\n 63.80 -1.39 (-2.13%) \n\n At close: October 23 at 4:00:01 PM EDT \n\n 63.80 0.00 (0.00%) \n\nAfter hours: October 23 at 5:21:48 PM EDT \n\n## Trading disclosure \n\n### -0.17%\n\n### 3.84%\n\n## What's happening with MDLZ?\n\nMondelez is approaching its October 29, 2026, third-quarter earnings report with expectations for modest revenue growth but lower earnings per share. Recent product launches and renewed marketing for nostalgic cookie brands support the company’s growth strategy, while cocoa costs, consumer trade-downs, debt, and dividend coverage remain key concerns.\n\n What does MDLZ's gluten-free Ritz launch add to growth? What does MDLZ's negative Earnings ESP say about Q3? Which analysts currently rate MDLZ at Buy or Overweight? Why is MDLZ down 2.1% in today's session? What are MDLZ's plans for the full-year dividend payout? \n\n## Mondelez International, Inc. Overview Confectioners / Consumer Defensive\n\nMondelez International, Inc., through its subsidiaries, manufactures, markets, and sells snack food and beverage products in Latin America, North America, Asia, the Middle East, Africa, and Europe. The company provides biscuits and baked snacks, including cookies, crackers, salted snacks, snack bars, and cakes and pastries; chocolates; and gums and candies, as well as various cheese, grocery, and powdered beverage products. Its brand portfolio includes Oreo, Ritz, LU, CLIF Bar, Tate's Bake Shop biscuits and baked snacks, Cadbury Dairy Milk, Milka, and Toblerone chocolate. It serves supermarket chains, wholesalers, supercenters, club stores, mass merchandisers, distributors, convenience stores, gasoline stations, drug stores, value stores, and other retail food outlets through direct store delivery, company-owned and satellite warehouses, distribution centers, third-party distributors, and other facilities, as well as through independent sales offices and agents. The company also sells products directly to businesses and consumers through e-retail platforms, retailer digital platforms, as well as through its direct-to-consumer websites and social media platforms. Mondelez International, Inc. was formerly known as Kraft Foods Inc. and changed its name to Mondelez International, Inc. in October 2012. The company was incorporated in 2000 and is headquartered in Chicago, Illinois.\n\nwww.mondelezinternational.com \n\n91,000\n\n### Full Time Employees\n\nDecember 31\n\n### Fiscal Year Ends\n\nConsumer Defensive \n\n### Sector\n\nConfectioners \n\n### Industry\n\n### Mondelez International’s 3.4% Yield: How Safe is the Payout?\n\nInsider Monkey • 8d ago\n\n### Performance Overview\n\nTrailing total returns as of 10/23/2026, which may include dividends or other distributions. Benchmark is S&P 500 (^GSPC) .\n\n### YTD Return\n\nMDLZ 21.62% \n\nS&P 500 (^GSPC) 11.37%\n\n### 1-Year Return\n\nMDLZ 8.29% \n\nS&P 500 (^GSPC) 13.14%\n\n### 3-Year Return\n\nMDLZ 9.04% \n\nS&P 500 (^GSPC) 80.78%\n\n### 5-Year Return\n\nMDLZ 20.70% \n\nS&P 500 (^GSPC) 67.74%\n\n### Revenue vs. Earnings \n\nRevenue 9.36B\n\nEarnings 1.55B\n\nProfit Margin 16.55%\n\nQ3\n\nFY25\n\nQ4\n\nFY25\n\nQ1\n\nFY26\n\nQ2\n\nFY26\n\n10.0% \n\n12.0% \n\nTop Analyst \n\nMizuho\n\n57/100\n\nOutperform \n\n### Analyst Price Targets\n\n55.00 Low\n\n69.09 Average\n\n63.80 Current\n\n77.00 High\n\n### Analyst Recommendations\n\n Strong Buy Buy Hold Underperform Sell \n\nDate 10/16/2026 Analyst Barclays Rating Action Maintains Rating Overweight Price Action Raises Price Target 70 -> 72\n\nAs of 9/22/2026\n\n- Market Cap \n\n77.96B \n- Enterprise Value \n\n98.30B \n- Trailing P/E \n\n22.37 \n- Forward P/E \n\n18.18 \n- PEG Ratio (5yr expected) \n\n0.90 \n- Price/Sales (ttm)\n\n1.99 \n- Price/Book (mrq)\n\n2.93 \n- Enterprise Value/Revenue \n\n2.48 \n- Enterprise Value/EBITDA \n\n15.66 \n\n#### Profitability and Income Statement\n\n- Profit Margin \n\n8.86%\n- Return on Assets (ttm)\n\n4.12%\n- Return on Equity (ttm)\n\n13.34%\n- Revenue (ttm)\n\n39.67B\n- Net Income Avi to Common (ttm)\n\n3.52B\n- Diluted EPS (ttm)\n\n2.72\n\n#### Balance Sheet and Cash Flow\n\n- Total Cash (mrq)\n\n1.72B\n- Total Debt/Equity (mrq)\n\n82.64%\n- Levered Free Cash Flow (ttm)\n\n2.25B\n\nSelect to analyze similar companies using key performance metrics; select up to 4 stocks.\n\nChocoladefabriken Lindt & Sprüngli AG\n\n+9.29% \n\nMkt Cap 23.412B Industry Confectioners\n\n### Fair Value\n\nFair Value is the appropriate price for the shares of a company, based on its earnings and growth rate also interpreted as when P/E Ratio = Growth Rate. Estimated return represents the projected annual return you might expect after purchasing shares in the company and holding them over the default time horizon of 5 years, based on the EPS growth rate that we have projected. Learn more \n\n63.80 Current\n\n### Dividend Score\n\nThe distribution of earnings a company pays back to its shareholders based off the company’s profits. Usually paid out on a quarterly basis in the form of cash or additional stocks.\n\nSector Avg.\n\n100 High\n\n### Hiring Score\n\nA company’s hiring trends in key indicators can indicate how well the company is doing in terms of growing the company and where they are currently prioritizing.\n\nSector Avg.\n\n100 High\n\n### Insider Sentiment Score\n\nVicker’s Insider Sentiment provides insights into trades made by insiders. This can be used to gain valuable insights and as a reflection of the prospect of the company based on insider trading.\n\nSector Avg.\n\n100 High\n\n- ### Stepped Up Brand Investments Support Mondelez's Competitive Prowess\n\nMondelez has operated independently since its split from the former Kraft Foods North American grocery business in October 2012. Since that time, the firm has been a leading player in the global snack enclave with a presence in the biscuit (48% of sales as of the end of fiscal 2025), chocolate (33%), gum/candy (10%), beverage (3%), and cheese and grocery (6%) aisles. Mondelez's portfolio includes well-known brands like Oreo, Chips Ahoy, Halls, and Cadbury. The firm garners around one-third of its revenue from developing markets, around 40% from Europe, and more than one-fourth from North America. \n\nMorningstar• 3 days ago \n- ### Stepped Up Brand Investments Support Mondelez's Competitive Prowess\n\nMondelez has operated independently since its split from the former Kraft Foods North American grocery business in October 2012. Since that time, the firm has been a leading player in the global snack enclave with a presence in the biscuit (48% of sales as of the end of fiscal 2025), chocolate (33%), gum/candy (10%), beverage (3%), and cheese and grocery (6%) aisles. Mondelez's portfolio includes well-known brands like Oreo, Chips Ahoy, Halls, and Cadbury. The firm garners around one-third of its revenue from developing markets, around 40% from Europe, and more than one-fourth from North America. \n\nMorningstar• 23 days ago \n- ### Looking for a more favorable entry point\n\nMondelez International Inc., headquartered in the Chicago suburb of Deerfield, Illinois, is a leading snack food and beverage company. Mondelez sells products in over 150 countries and has operations in more than 80 countries and production facilities in 46 countries. The company holds many well-known brands, including Oreo and Chips Ahoy! cookies, Ritz crackers, belVita biscuits, Cadbury Dairy Milk, Clif, and Toblerone. The company has approximately 91,000 employees. The shares are a component of the S&P 500.\n\nArgus• 2 months ago \n- ### This is the biggest earnings week of this reporting season by volume, with\n\nThis is the biggest earnings week of this reporting season by volume, with nearly 1,600 public companies on the list. It's also a jobs week. The major indices -- especially the Nasdaq -- had a volatile last week. The Dow Jones Industrial Average and the S&P 500 both ended up 1%, while the Nasdaq gained about 1.5%. Year to date, all three indices are in positive territory and all are up by about 9%. On the earnings calendar, headliners include Palantir Technologies and Marriott on Monday; SpaceX, AMD, Caterpillar, Merck, and McDonald's on Tuesday; Eli Lilly, Walt Disney, Sandisk, Uber, and CVS Health on Wednesday; ConocoPhillips, Parker-Hannifin, and Constellation Energy on Thursday; and Berkshire Hathaway on Friday. Some 42% of S&P 500 companies have reported as of last week. Earnings so far are up a whopping 40% from last quarter, with Energy and Communication Services leading the charge and Healthcare at the bottom. On the economic calendar, the jobs report for July comes out on Friday. Ahead of that, several other labor indicators will hit the tape, including the Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey (JOLTS) and the ADP employment report. Turing to economic forecasts, gas prices ticked up last week, rising 10 cents to an average of $4.10 per gallon for regular gas. Elsewhere, the Atlanta Fed GDPNow forecasts call for 3Q GDP growth of 5.0% -- a big jump from the 1.5% reported last week for 2Q. The Cleveland Fed Inflation Nowcast forecasts is for CPI of 3.4% in July, unchanged from last week's forecast. Mortgage rates rose another eight basis points last week, with the average 30-year fixed-rate mortgage now at 6.66%, according to FreddieMac. The next Federal Open Market Committee rate decision is on September 16. Odds are at 63% for a rate hike at that meeting, up from 37% last week. Taking a deeper dive into performance so far in 2026, a leading industrialized global stock market index, the ETF EFA, is up 10% year to date, while the leading emerging market ETF (EEM) is up 17%. U.S. growth stocks are down 1% looking at ETF IWF, while value stocks (IWD) are up 19%. Crude oil prices continue to be volatile. On Friday, oil closed at $85 per barrel and is up 46% for the year to date. In other asset classes for the year to date, AGG bonds are down 3%, gold is down 7%, and Bitcoin is down 28%. The U.S. dollar is up 2%, tracking DXY. The VIX Volatility Index was at about 17 on Friday, below its historical average of 20. Turning to sector performance, the list from first to worst so far in 2026 is Energy (+32%), Information Technology (+22%), Industrials (+15%), Materials (+14%), Real Estate (+12%), Consumer Staples (+10%), Healthcare (+6%), Utilities (+5%), Financials (+4%), Consumer Discretionary (-6%), and Communication Services (-10%).\n\nArgus• 2 months ago","title":"Mondelez International, Inc. (MDLZ) Stock Price, News, Quote & History - Yahoo Finance","url":"https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/MDLZ/"},{"author":null,"id":"https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/CP/","text":"Canadian Pacific Kansas City Limited (CP) Stock Price, News, Quote & History - Yahoo Finance\n\nNYSE - BOATS Real Time Price • USD \n\n# Canadian Pacific Kansas City Limited (CP)\n\n 90.62 +2.52 (+2.86%) \n\n At close: October 22 at 4:00:02 PM EDT \n\n 90.22 -0.40 (-0.44%) \n\nOvernight: 8:00:49 PM EDT\n\nThis price reflects trading activity during the overnight session on the Blue Ocean ATS, available 8 PM to 4 AM ET, Sunday through Thursday, when regular markets are closed. \n\n## Trading disclosure \n\n### 4.27%\n\n### 2.99%\n\n### 10.12%\n\nChart does not reflect overnight price.\n\n- Previous Close \n\n88.10 \n- Open \n\n88.09 \n- Bid --\n- Ask --\n- Day's Range \n\n87.85 - 91.21 \n- 52 Week Range \n\n68.42 - 96.90 \n- Volume \n\n4,199,165 \n- Avg. Volume \n\n2,341,895 \n- Market Cap (intraday) \n\n79.662B \n- Beta (5Y Monthly) 1.22\n- PE Ratio (TTM) \n\n29.14 \n- EPS (TTM) \n\n3.11 \n- Earnings Date Oct 28, 2026\n- Forward Dividend & Yield 0.76 (0.84%)\n- 1y Target Est \n\n99.36 \n- Morningstar Rating \n\nRating available with Stock Picks access\n\n## Canadian Pacific Kansas City Limited Overview Railroads / Industrials\n\nCanadian Pacific Kansas City Limited, together with its subsidiaries, owns and operates a transcontinental freight railway in Canada, the United States, and Mexico. The transports bulk commodities, including grain, coal, potash, fertilizers, and sulphur; merchandise freight consists of industrial and consumer products, such as forest products, energy, chemicals and plastics, metals, minerals, consumer products, and automotive; and intermodal traffic comprising retail goods in overseas containers. The company also provides rail and intermodal transportation services through a network of approximately 20,000 miles serving business centers. The company was formerly known as Canadian Pacific Railway Limited and changed its name to Canadian Pacific Kansas City Limited in April 2023. Canadian Pacific Kansas City Limited was founded in 1881 and is headquartered in Calgary, Canada.\n\nwww.cpkcr.com \n\n19,687\n\n### Full Time Employees\n\nDecember 31\n\n### Fiscal Year Ends\n\nIndustrials \n\n### Sector\n\nRailroads \n\n### Industry\n\n### Performance Overview\n\nTrailing total returns as of 10/22/2026, which may include dividends or other distributions. Benchmark is S&P/TSX Composite index (^GSPTSE) .\n\n### YTD Return\n\nCP 22.77% \n\nS&P/TSX Composite index (^GSPTSE) 12.89%\n\n### 1-Year Return\n\nCP 19.22% \n\nS&P/TSX Composite index (^GSPTSE) 19.98%\n\n### 3-Year Return\n\nCP 30.06% \n\nS&P/TSX Composite index (^GSPTSE) 88.19%\n\n### 5-Year Return\n\nCP 23.74% \n\nS&P/TSX Composite index (^GSPTSE) 69.56%\n\n### Revenue vs. Earnings \n\nRevenue 4.16B\n\nEarnings 1.02B\n\nProfit Margin 24.59%\n\nQ3\n\nFY25\n\nQ4\n\nFY25\n\nQ1\n\nFY26\n\nQ2\n\nFY26\n\n3B \n\n4B \n\n23.0% \n\n24.0% \n\n25.0% \n\n26.0% \n\n27.0% \n\n### Analyst Price Targets\n\n75.09 Low\n\n99.36 Average\n\n90.62 Current\n\n106.57 High\n\n### Analyst Recommendations\n\n Strong Buy Buy Hold Underperform Sell \n\n### Latest Rating\n\nDate 10/7/2026 Analyst Citigroup Rating Action Maintains Rating Buy Price Action Lowers Price Target 109 -> 102\n\nAs of 9/23/2026\n\n- Market Cap \n\n77.80B \n- Enterprise Value \n\n95.42B \n- Trailing P/E \n\n28.64 \n- Forward P/E \n\n20.75 \n- PEG Ratio (5yr expected) \n\n-- \n- Price/Sales (ttm)\n\n7.16 \n- Price/Book (mrq)\n\n2.32 \n- Enterprise Value/Revenue \n\n8.69 \n- Enterprise Value/EBITDA \n\n16.49 \n\n#### Profitability and Income Statement\n\n- Profit Margin \n\n25.03%\n- Return on Assets (ttm)\n\n4.44%\n- Return on Equity (ttm)\n\n8.15%\